## What a `type: predicate` instance is

**A predicate memorial describes a single typed-edge predicate** — one of the vocabulary terms (`supersedes`, `evidences`, `refines`, etc.) that authors use in frontmatter to declare typed edges between memorials.

The predicate memorial is the **authoritative schema** for its predicate: it declares what source + target types are allowed (`domain` / `range`), what formal characteristics hold (transitive, symmetric, etc.), what its inverse-of mate is, and what the audit layer should enforce.

Tooling that works with typed edges reads the predicate memorial at resolve-time.  No separate config file governs the predicate vocabulary — the predicate memorials ARE the vocabulary.

Predicate is a subtype of [`meta`](meta.md) — memorials about the memory system itself.

## Why predicates are first-class

**RDFS's foundational move**: `rdf:Property` is itself a `rdfs:Class`.  Every property in an RDF graph is a resource with its own URI, domain, range, and describable properties-of-properties.  This corpus inherits that discipline: every predicate in the typed-edge vocabulary is itself a memorial, in the same corpus, described with the same vocabulary, audited by the same invariants as any other memorial.

Four levels of metacircularity:

- **Memorials** describe things
- **Type memorials** (`type: type`) describe what memorials of a given type contain
- **Predicate memorials** (`type: predicate`) describe what typed edges between memorials mean
- **The Predicate type itself** describes what predicate memorials contain

## When to create a predicate memorial

- A new typed-edge relationship enters the vocabulary
- An existing predicate's semantics change (rare — prefer creating a new predicate to changing an old one)
- A relationship used only in one context turns out to deserve a vocabulary slot (promotion from `related:` fallback)

## When NOT to create one

- The relationship is covered by an existing predicate; reuse + refine docstring rather than proliferate
- The relationship is genuinely weak / associative and fits under `related:` (SKOS-style catch-all)
- The relationship exists only between one pair of memorials; predicates are schema, not one-off cross-links

