## Canonical body structure

```markdown
## What this predicate means

<One-paragraph semantics in plain language.>

## Formal signature

`<predicate> : <Domain> × <Range> → Bool`

<Any type-family notation, category-theoretic notes, etc.>

## Characteristics

<Enumerate the declared characteristics + any that derive from them.>

## Examples from the corpus

<2-3 concrete cases.  Format: `A <predicate> B` with memorial
rel-paths.  If the predicate is new enough that corpus examples
don't exist, use hypothetical examples.>

## Audit consequences

<What invariants fire on edges using this predicate.>

## Relationship to adjacent predicates

<Inverse, sub-predicates, contrast with near-siblings.>

## Migration guidance

<Heuristics for edge migration:
- Reach for this when ...
- Prefer stronger predicate when ...
- Stay with `related` when ...>

## Lifecycle

<Current lifecycle-status; history of status changes; migration
target + timeline if deprecated/retired.>

## See also

<Other predicates in the same cluster; research substrate;
patterns / plans using this predicate heavily.>
```

## Audit invariants that operate on predicate memorials

- **`predicate-referential-integrity`** — every predicate key used in any memorial's frontmatter resolves to an existing predicate memorial
- **`predicate-domain-range-conformance`** — for each typed edge `(A, p, B)`, `type(A) ∈ p.domain` and `type(B) ∈ p.range`
- **`predicate-characteristics-enforced`** — declared characteristics are upheld on the typed-edge graph (transitive closure / acyclicity / etc.)
- **`predicate-inverse-integrity`** — if `p.inverse-of = q`, then `(A p B)` implies `(B q A)` is asserted or derivable

## Pitfalls

- **Over-specifying domain / range** — types are already strict; over-restricting means authors can't express real relationships.  Start broad; tighten when real misuse patterns emerge
- **Hand-wavy characteristics** — `:transitive` is a mathematical claim.  If audit can't verify the closure cleanly, the predicate isn't actually transitive
- **Creating a new predicate instead of reusing** — check existing vocabulary first
- **Forgetting the inverse** — most asymmetric predicates have an inverse; declare both with `inverse-of` cross-pointing
- **Cluttered body** — predicate memorials should be concise (~150 lines max); write schemas, not essays

